35,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"It appears my suspicions were well-founded. While some structural elements hold up, others have decayed with surprising haste. Furthermore, the cost of maintaining greenery is escalating at a rate that would make any accountant shudder.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Misaki,,"My findings are concerning. Only two regional firms are tendering for earth-moving, and both have flagged risks regarding clay subsoil and slippage. We must be cautious about any plan requiring extensive excavation.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Mio,,"Whoa, Benjamin is right! I saw that too. The cost for planted areas is way higher than hard surfaces, and it's just getting worse every year. We really can't afford a plan that's too fancy to maintain!","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Asuka,,"The staffing situation is critical. The groundskeeping roll has plummeted from eighteen posts to seven. We are relying heavily on bought-in services and volunteers, which suggests the internal capacity to manage new assets is severely diminished.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Minako,,"The physical reality is equally concerning. People are carving their own paths to the tram, ignoring the margins, and our drainage is failing. Furthermore, the heat island effect is significant. We cannot ignore these environmental stresses.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Asuka, @Minako, it seems we are painting a rather bleak portrait of our current capabilities. We have failing drainage, a skeleton crew, and a history of superficial finishes. A ""grand vision"" without a maintenance plan is merely a future ruin.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Asuka"",""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Misaki,,"@Benjamin, I agree. Given the slippage risks and the limited number of capable firms, any plan with ambitious earth-works or complex phasing will likely face delays and cost overruns. We need something structurally simple.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Mio,,"Exactly! If we're already struggling with a tiny crew and costs are skyrocketing for the greenery, we can't just pick the prettiest plan. We need something that won't break the bank in three years!","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Mio, it is not just about the budget. With only seven posts remaining on the roll, we lack the human capital to oversee complex projects. Any plan requiring intensive daily care is fundamentally unrealistic.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Minako,,"@Asuka, exactly. We must prioritize a design that accommodates the actual flow of pedestrians toward the tram and addresses the drainage failures. A plan that ignores these physical pressures will fail regardless of the budget.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"It seems we are in rare agreement. We require a plan that is structurally modest, respects the actual pedestrian flow, and avoids an over-reliance on high-maintenance greenery. Simplicity, in this case, is the height of sophistication.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Misaki,,I agree. We should prioritize a plan that minimizes deep excavation to avoid the clay subsoil risks and allows for a straightforward construction phase that doesn't paralyze the main gate. Practicality must come before aesthetics.,"{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Mio,,"I totally agree! Let's look for a plan that's easy to take care of and doesn't need a huge team. If it's simple and sturdy, it'll stay beautiful without costing us a fortune later!","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Mio, precisely. We must avoid any design that assumes a full staff. If the plan requires constant pruning or intricate gardening, it will simply be neglected, as we have no one left to do the work.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Minako,,"@Asuka, I agree. We must also ensure the plan doesn't fight the land. If we ignore the drainage issues and the heat, the materials will degrade quickly, adding more pressure to an already strained workforce.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,"It seems we have successfully stripped away the illusions. We are looking for a plan that is durable, low-maintenance, and honest about the site's constraints. I believe we are now well-equipped to judge the candidates.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Misaki,,"I agree. We have a clear set of criteria now: minimal excavation, low maintenance, and a realistic construction phase. I look forward to seeing which candidate best aligns with these practical constraints.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Mio,,I'm ready! Let's find the plan that's actually doable. I'll be keeping a super close eye on those maintenance costs so we don't end up with a money pit!,"{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Asuka,,I will be evaluating the candidates based on their labor requirements. Any plan that assumes a level of staffing we no longer possess is a failure in the making. We need a design that is truly self-sustaining.,"{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Minako,,"I will evaluate the candidates based on their resilience to environmental stress and their alignment with actual pedestrian movement. We must choose a plan that works with the land, not against it.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
